    "authorAssociation": "NONE",
    "body": "For example, I'd like to put the word under the cursor and the following 3 words within a pair of quotation marks. Can I do that?",
    "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">For example, I'd like to put the word under the cursor and the following 3 words within a pair of quotation marks. Can I do that?</p>",
    "bodyText": "For example, I'd like to put the word under the cursor and the following 3 words within a pair of quotation marks. Can I do that?",
    "category": {
        "createdAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z",
        "description": "Ask the community for help",
        "emoji": ":pray:",
        "emojiHTML": "<div>🙏</div>",
        "id": "DIC_kwDOFoksYM4CAgqN",
        "isAnswerable": true,
        "name": "Q&A",
        "slug": "q-a",
        "updatedAt": "2022-01-02T14:24:09Z"
    },
    "closed": true,
    "closedAt": "2024-07-10T08:01:36Z",
    "comment_count": 1,
    "comment_data": [
        {
            "author": {
                "avatarUrl": "https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/747855?v=4",
                "login": "pkazmier",
                "resourcePath": "/pkazmier",
                "url": "https://github.com/pkazmier"
            },
            "authorAssociation": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Sure, there are several ways you can do that with standard vim motions:\r\n\r\n1. Assuming your cursor is on the start of the first word, you can press `sa4e\"`, which will add a surrounding (`sa`) around the current position and the end of the next four words (`4e`) using quotation marks (`\"`). If you are unsure of how many words, you could do `veeeesa\"`, which will give you visual feedback on the words you are going to place quotes around.\r\n2. If your cursor was not at the start of the first word, but in the middle, you could use the \"around word\" textobject, but you'll have to back off by one character, so you don't quote an empty space at the end. Because you need to adjust the selection, you'll want to use visual mode by pressing `v4awhsa\"` or the more interactive version `vawawawawhsa\"`. \r\n\r\nI'm sure there are many other ways to do the same, but that should get you pointed in the right direction. ",
            "bodyHTML": "<p dir=\"auto\">Sure, there are several ways you can do that with standard vim motions:</p>\n<ol dir=\"auto\">\n<li>Assuming your cursor is on the start of the first word, you can press <code class=\"notranslate\">sa4e\"</code>, which will add a surrounding (<code class=\"notranslate\">sa</code>) around the current position and the end of the next four words (<code class=\"notranslate\">4e</code>) using quotation marks (<code class=\"notranslate\">\"</code>). If you are unsure of how many words, you could do <code class=\"notranslate\">veeeesa\"</code>, which will give you visual feedback on the words you are going to place quotes around.</li>\n<li>If your cursor was not at the start of the first word, but in the middle, you could use the \"around word\" textobject, but you'll have to back off by one character, so you don't quote an empty space at the end. Because you need to adjust the selection, you'll want to use visual mode by pressing <code class=\"notranslate\">v4awhsa\"</code> or the more interactive version <code class=\"notranslate\">vawawawawhsa\"</code>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p dir=\"auto\">I'm sure there are many other ways to do the same, but that should get you pointed in the right direction.</p>",
            "bodyText": "Sure, there are several ways you can do that with standard vim motions:\n\nAssuming your cursor is on the start of the first word, you can press sa4e\", which will add a surrounding (sa) around the current position and the end of the next four words (4e) using quotation marks (\"). If you are unsure of how many words, you could do veeeesa\", which will give you visual feedback on the words you are going to place quotes around.\nIf your cursor was not at the start of the first word, but in the middle, you could use the \"around word\" textobject, but you'll have to back off by one character, so you don't quote an empty space at the end. Because you need to adjust the selection, you'll want to use visual mode by pressing v4awhsa\" or the more interactive version vawawawawhsa\".\n\nI'm sure there are many other ways to do the same, but that should get you pointed in the right direction.",
